{
    "uri": "https://openalex.org/W7129758400",
    "title": "Data-driven Approaches based on Cross-scale Dense Convolutional Network for Detecting Climate Change Effects on Crop Yield and Farming Patterns",
    "venue": {
        "name": "International journal of intelligent engineering and systems",
        "id": "https://openalex.org/S2764379787",
        "type": "journal"
    },
    "publication_year": 2026,
    "authors": [],
    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.22266/ijies2026.0331.47",
    "abstract": "Climate stress is reshaping crop productivity in the Himalayan region, yet decision support is often fragmented: climate forecasting, vulnerability mapping, and agricultural-growth attribution are usually treated as separate tasks.This paper presents an end-to-end framework that (i) standardizes exposure, sensitivity, and adaptivecapacity indicators to quantify vulnerability across 22 districts in Jammu & Kashmir and Ladakh, (ii) augments multichannel climate records using a GAN, and (iii) forecasts key climate drivers (precipitation, temperature extremes, and relative humidity) with a Cross-Scale Dense Convolutional Network trained via ADMM-based updates.Unlike prior studies that apply forecasting or vulnerability scoring in isolation, the same pipeline links district-wise vulnerability indices, climate projections, and Ricardian growth estimation to deliver actionable rankings and factor sensitivities.Beyond combining components, the proposed framework couples GAN-augmented multichannel climate forecasting with an ADMM-stabilized CSDC-Net and then propagates the same forecasts into vulnerability ranking and Ricardian factor-sensitivity (growth attribution), producing district-wise, policy-actionable insights rather than standalone prediction scores.Experiments combine 42 socioeconomic/agronomic indicators with NASA POWER exposure variables and derived climate-change indicators.The proposed CSDC-Net-ADMM achieves an overall accuracy of R = 0.892 with low prediction errors (RMSE = 0.185, MAE = 0.170), outperforming common deep baselines such as LSTM, RNN, and 1D-CNN.The vulnerability assessment reveals marked spatial disparities, identifying higher-risk districts (e.g., Budgam and Bandipora) and lower-risk districts (e.g., Kargil and Rajouri).The integrated analysis further indicates that rising minimum temperature and shrinking landholdings reduce agricultural productivity, whereas improved literacy and credit access strengthen adaptive capacity and help mitigate vulnerability.These outputs support climate-resilient planning by prioritizing interventions, allocation, and early warning of yield risks at district scale.",
    "field": "Climate change and agriculture",
    "problem": "Existing approaches for assessing climate change's agricultural impact are fragmented, suffering from data scarcity, weak modeling of variable dependencies, and a disconnect between climate forecasts and actionable, district-wise policy outputs, which hinders integrated planning and decision support.",
    "method": "The paper proposes an end-to-end framework using a GAN-augmented Cross-Scale Dense Convolutional Network to forecast climate drivers and integrate these predictions into vulnerability and Ricardian analyses, providing actionable insights for district-level agricultural planning.",
    "task": "The task is to develop an end-to-end framework using a GAN and a Cross-Scale Dense Convolutional Network to forecast climate drivers, assess agricultural vulnerability, and analyze crop yield impacts in the Himalayan region to support climate-resilient planning.",
